# Git Cheat Sheet

Important Git commands from Week 2 — focused on useful, non-basic commands.

## History & Changes

```bash
git log --oneline --graph --all
```
Visualize compact commit history across all branches.

```bash
git diff
```
Show changes that are not staged.

```bash
git diff --staged
```
Show changes that are staged but not committed.

```bash
git diff <hash1> <hash2>
```
Compare changes between two commits.

```bash
git diff HEAD~2 HEAD
```
Compare the current commit with the commit two positions before it.

```bash
git show <commit-hash>
```
Show the details and changes introduced by a specific commit.

```bash
git log origin/main --oneline
```
Show the commits currently recorded on the remote-tracking `origin/main` branch.

```bash
git log --oneline <branch>
```
Show compact commit history for a specific branch.

## Selective Staging

```bash
git add -p
```
Interactively stage only selected chunks of your changes.

## Remotes

```bash
git remote -v
```
Show the configured remote repositories and their URLs.

```bash
git remote add origin <url>
```
Add a remote repository named `origin`.

## Fetch & Pull

```bash
git fetch origin
```
Download changes from `origin` without merging them.

```bash
git fetch --all
```
Download changes from all configured remotes.

```bash
git pull
```
Fetch remote changes and merge them into the current branch.

```bash
git merge origin/main
```
Merge the fetched `origin/main` changes into your current branch.

## Branches

```bash
git branch -a
```
List local branches and remote-tracking branches.

```bash
git checkout -b <branch>
```
Create a new branch and switch to it.

```bash
git checkout <branch>
```
Switch to an existing branch.

```bash
git branch -d <branch>
```
Safely delete a local branch that has been merged.

## Push

```bash
git push origin <branch>
```
Push a local branch to the matching branch on `origin`.

```bash
git push origin <local>:<remote>
```
Push a local branch to a differently named remote branch.

## Merge

```bash
git merge <branch>
```
Merge another branch into the current branch.

## Rebase

```bash
git rebase <branch>
```
Replay the current branch's commits on top of another branch.

```bash
git rebase -i HEAD~3
```
Interactively edit, reorder, squash, or reword the last three commits.

```bash
git rebase -i main
```
Interactively clean up the current branch before integrating it with `main`.

```bash
git rebase --continue
```
Continue a rebase after resolving a conflict.

```bash
git pull --rebase origin main
```
Fetch and replay your local commits on top of the latest `main`.

## Cherry-pick

```bash
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
```
Apply one specific commit from another branch to the current branch.

## Shallow Clone

```bash
git clone --depth=1 <url>
```
Clone only the latest snapshot without the full commit history.

## Amend Commits

```bash
git commit --amend -m "corrected message"
```
Replace the message of the most recent commit.

```bash
git commit --amend --no-edit
```
Add staged changes to the most recent commit while keeping its message.

```bash
git commit --amend --author="Name <email>"
```
Change the author of the most recent commit.

## Search History

```bash
git log -p --all | grep "<text>"
```
Search all commit patches for specific text.

```bash
git log -S "<text>"
```
Find commits where a specific string was added or removed.

## Tracking Files

```bash
git rm --cached <file>
```
Stop tracking a file while keeping it on your local disk.

```bash
git rm <file>
```
Remove a file from the working tree and stage its deletion.

## Remove Files from History

```bash
pip install git-filter-repo
```
Install the `git-filter-repo` history-rewriting tool.

```bash
git filter-repo --path <file> --invert-paths
```
Remove a file from the repository's entire Git history.

## Stash

```bash
git stash
```
Temporarily save uncommitted changes and clean the working tree.

```bash
git stash pop
```
Restore the latest stash and remove it from the stash list.

```bash
git stash list
```
List all saved stashes.

```bash
git stash push -m "wip"
```
Create a named stash for your current uncommitted changes.

```bash
git stash apply stash@{n}
```
Apply a specific stash without removing it.

```bash
git stash drop stash@{n}
```
Delete a specific stash.

## Force Push

```bash
git push --force origin <branch>
```
Force the remote branch to match rewritten local history.

```bash
git push --force-with-lease origin <branch>
```
Force-push more safely by checking that the remote has not changed unexpectedly.

## Configuration

```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
```
Set your global Git username.

```bash
git config --global user.email "you@email.com"
```
Set your global Git email.

```bash
git config user.email "email@example.com"
```
Set the Git email for the current repository only.

---

## ⭐ Key Rules

- Prefer `git fetch` + inspect over blindly using `git pull`.
- Rebase your own local feature branches; avoid rebasing shared branches.
- Prefer `--force-with-lease` over `--force` when force-pushing.
- Never commit secrets; deleting them later does not remove them from Git history.
- `git rm --cached` untracks a file but keeps it on disk.
- `git cherry-pick` copies one specific commit as a new commit.
